      "math_academy_id": "e-367",
      "problem": "Find the antiderivative of  $\\sqrt x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by writing the square root as a rational power, $\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
